
{smcl}
{cmd:help pvar2}
{right: 06July2012, Lian Yujun (arlion)}
{hline}

{title:Title}

{p2colset 5 20 22 2}{...}
{p2col :{hi:[XT] pvar2} {hline 2}}Panel Vector Autoregressive Models{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}


{title:Syntax}

{p 8 14 4}{cmd:pvar2} {depvarlist} [if]
[{cmd:,} 
{cmdab:l:ag:(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
{cmdab:irf:(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
{cmdab:irff:ormat:}{cmd:(%}{it:{help format:fmt}}{cmd:)}
{cmdab:r:eps:(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
{cmdab:d:ecomp:(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
{cmdab:no:graph}
{cmdab:res:idual}
{cmdab:s:aving:(}{it:filename}{cmd:)}
{cmdab:t:imeeffect}
{cmdab:soc}
{cmdab:g:ranger}
{cmdab:seed(}{it:#}{cmd:)}
]

{synoptset 18 tabbed}{...}
{synopthdr}
{synoptline}
{syntab:Model}
  {synopt :{opt lag(#)}}specifies the number of lags in the underlying VAR; 
default is 1; {it:#} has to be integer >0. Note that, when option {opt granger} 
is specified, {it:#} must be no less than 2.{p_end}
  {synopt :{opt irf(#)}}calculate the impulse response function and 
generates error bands for impulse-responses using monte-carlo simulations. 
{opt #} specifies the steps in calculating the impulse-responses.{p_end}
  {synopt:{opt irfformat}{cmd:(%}{it:{help format:fmt}}{cmd:)}}
display format for y-axis labels in the IRF graphs; default format is {cmd:%6.3f}.{p_end}
  {synopt :{opt reps(#)}}The default number of repetitions of Monte Carlo simuliation is 200. 
If you want change the repwtitions, just specify the number in {opt reps(#)}.{p_end} 
  {synopt :{opt nograph}}By default when {opt irf(#)} is specified, the IRFs graphs will be drawn.
Specify {opt nograph} will not display the IRFs graphs, but the IRFs dataset 
will still by saved.{p_end} 
  {synopt :{opt residual}} When this option is specified, 
the variance-covrance and correlation matrix of
residuals will be presented.{p_end}
  {synopt :{opt decomp(#)}} generates a table containing the results of 
Forecast-error Variance Decompositions (FEVD). {opt #} is the max. number of periods.{p_end}
  {synopt :{opt saving(filename)}} specifies the filename of the dataset generated by 
{opt irf(#)}, which contains the Monte Carlo simulation results of IRFs and 
their 95% confidence intervals. 
If {opt saving(filename)} is not specified, the IRFs dataset will by saved in the 
current working directory (see {help pwd}), named as "irf_data.dta". 
You can list the data files by typing command "dir *.dta" after {help pvar2} with {opt irf(#)}.{p_end}
  {synopt :{opt timeeffect} } demeans the time effects of the original variables before they are 
helmert-transformed. The transformation is in spirit of {cmd:xtdata,fe} (see help {help xtdata}). 
For variable x[it], the time-demeaned variable is {p_end}

{p 32 4 2}
x[it]* = x[it] - mean_x[t] + mean_x, 
{p_end}

{p 26 26 0}where, mean_x[t] is the mean of x[it] at time (year or month) {it:t}, and 
mean_x is the sample mean of x[it].{p_end}

{synopt :{opt soc}} reports the Akaike's information criterion
    (AIC), Schwartz's Bayesian information criterion (BIC), and the Hannan and Quinn
    information criterion (HQIC) lag-order selection statistics for a series of panel
    vector autoregressions of order 1,..,lag(#). The smallest criterion is marked with a
    "*".{p_end}
  {synopt :{opt granger}} performs a set of Granger causality tests for each equation
in a PVAR, providing a convenient alternative to {help test}. 
Note that, when specify this 
option, a PVAR(2) of higher order PVAR model is more feasible. So, specify 
{opt lag(#)} (with # >= 2) accompanied with {opt granger}.{p_end}
  {synopt :{opt seed(#)}}set random-number {help seed} to # in Monte Carlo simulation 
of IRFs error bounds. {p_end} 
{synoptline}
{p 4 4 2}You must {cmd:tsset} or {cmd:xtset} your data before using {cmd:pvar}, 
see help {help tsset} or {help xtset}.
{it:depvarlist} may contain time series operators. See {help depvarlist}.{p_end}


{title:Description}

{p 4 4 2}
{cmd:pvar2} estimates a PVAR model using GMM as described in Holtz et al. (1988). 
Some formulas in pvar2.ado (e.g. IRFs) are from Hamilton (1994, chp.11). 

{p 4 4 2}
{it:depvarlist} is the list with variable names in the desired order. 
Different orderings will yield different responses. 
{p_end}

{p 4 4 2}
Before you can run these you must do the command tsset to tell Stata what is your panel data structure.
For example, if your cross-section variable is named -id- and yur time variable is named -year-, 
do the command: tsset id year.

{error}{dlgtab:What is new}{text}

{p 4 4 2}
{cmd:pvar2} is a new version of {cmd:pvar} given by I. Love (World bank) which is run in Stata 6.0, 
while {cmd:pvar2} supports stata 8.0 or above. The main difference is that {cmd:pvar2} is more easy 
to use, and the graph is more beautiful. 

{p 4 4 2}
Moreover,  the data has to be helmert transformed (see Holtz et al., 1988) prior to estimation in order to remove 
the fixed effects when the old version {cmd:pvar} is used. However, 
in {cmd:pvar2}, this transfermation is build-in, so that you need not make any transformation of
your data before excuting {cmd:pvar2}.
The model uses untransformed variables as instruments for the helmert-transformed variables in the model. 

{p 4 4 2}
{cmd:pvar2} supports flexible specifications of {cmd:steps} in both IRFs (impulse response functions) and 
FEVD (forcast error variance decompositions) by using options {opt irf(#)} 
and {opt decomp(#)}, respectively. 

{p 4 4 2}
{cmd:pvar2} calculates {opt AIC}, {opt BIC}, and {opt HQIC}. 
These statistic values can also be saved in the estimation results. See {help estimate store} 
and {help esttab}.

{p 4 4 2}
{cmd:pvar2} now support {opt timeeffect} option, which can demean the time effects from the original variables
before helmert transformed.

{p 4 4 2}
{cmd:pvar2} also support {opt granger} option, which performs a set of Granger causality tests for each equation
in a PVAR.
  

{title:Examples}

{result}{dlgtab:Data setup}{text}

{phang2}{inp:.} {stata "webuse grunfeld,clear":webuse grunfeld, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{inp:. xtset company year}{p_end}


{result}{dlgtab:PVAR basic}{text}

{pstd} System-GMM estimation of PVAR(1) and PVAR(2), without IRFs: {p_end}

{phang2}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue}{p_end}
{phang2}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, lag(2)}{p_end}


{result}{dlgtab:Selection Order Criteria for Panel VAR}{text}

{pstd} Estimate a PVAR(5) model and select the most feasible lag order:{p_end}

{phang2}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, lag(5) soc}{p_end}


{result}{dlgtab:PVAR with IRFs}{text}

{pstd} System-GMM estimation with IRFs graphs (6-periods ahead), 
and Monte Carlo 200 times (the default value) to get error bounds: {p_end}

{phang2}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, lag(2) irf(6)}{p_end}

{pstd} Set {opt irfformat}{cmd:(%}{it:{help format:fmt}}{cmd:)} to improve the IRF graphs: {p_end}

{phang2}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, irf(6) irfformat(%4.0f)}{p_end}

{pstd} Monte Carlo 1000 times to get the error bounds of IRFs:{p_end}

{phang2}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, lag(2) irf(6) reps(1000)}{p_end}

{pstd} Saving the IRFs dataset in the current working directory named as myIRF.dta:{p_end}

{phang2}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, lag(2) irf(6) reps(1000) saving(myIRF)}{p_end}


{result}{dlgtab:Variance decomposition}{text}

{pstd} IRFs 5 steps and present the variance decomposition 10-steps:{p_end}

{phang2}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, irf(5) decomp(10) nograph}{p_end}


{result}{dlgtab:Granger Causality Tests}{text}

{pstd} Estimate a PVAR(3) model and perform Granger causality tests:{p_end}

{phang2}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, lag(3) granger}{p_end}


  
{title:Saved results and return values}


{result}{dlgtab:What PVAR2.ado saved?}{text}

{p 4 4 2}
{cmd:pavr2} saves many {cmd:matrix}, {cmd:.dta} files and {cmd:macro} in memory. 
We can use these return values to redraw the IRFs graphs, to restore results and so on.
{p_end}

{p 4 4 2}
After {cmd:pavr2}, 

{p 4 4 2}type {cmd: ereturn list} will list all return elements saved in memory.{p_end}

{p 4 4 2}type {cmd: matrix dir} will list all matrices saved in memory.{p_end}

{p 4 4 2}Type {cmd:macro dir} will list all macros. {p_end}

{p 4 4 2}Type {cmd:dir *.dta} will list all datasets saved by {cmd:pavr2} in the disk. {p_end}

{p 4 4 2}Type {cmd:dir gr*.gph} will list all graphs saved by {cmd:pavr2}.{p_end}


{result}{dlgtab:Graphing your own IRFs after PVAR2.ado}{text}

{p 4 4 2}
If you are unsatisfied with the IRFs-graphs given by {cmd:pvar2}, then the you can use the dataset 
saved by {cmd:pvar2} to redraw these graphs. The dataset {opt "irf_data.dta"} contains the results of impulse response values
and 5% and 95% error bounds.
{p_end}

{p 4 4} For example, after type: {p_end}

{p 4 4}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, lag(2) reps(1000) irf(10)}{p_end}

{p 4 4 2}
The dataset of IRFs automatically saved is "irf_data.dta". Type the following commands can draw a 
IRFs graph of variable {it:kstock} to {it:invest}:
{p_end}

{p 4 4} {inp:. use irf_data.dta, clear} {p_end}
{p 4 4} {inp:. twoway line invest_5 invest invest_95 s if varname=="kstock", ///} {p_end}
{p 13 4} {inp:        title("Impulse reponse of kstock to invest")           ///} {p_end}
{p 13 4} {inp:        xtitle(Steps) ylabel(,angle(0))                        ///} {p_end}
{p 13 4} {inp:        yline(0, lpattern(dash))                               ///} {p_end}
{p 13 4} {inp:        scheme(s1mono) legend(off)} {p_end}

{p 4 4 2}
You can also draw IRFs of kstock to mvalue, and kstock, 
and save these graphs to disk using {help graph save} command.
Then, they can be combined using {help graph combine} command.
{p_end}


{result}{dlgtab:A more easy way to reset IRFs after PVAR2.ado}{text}

{p 4 4} Note that, after {cmd:pvar2}, a list of graphs are automatically saved in your disk 
(the current working dictionary), named as gr#_#.gph. 
Type {cmd:dir gr*.gph} will list the names of these graphs. 
For example, after the following commad is excuted, "gr1_3.gph" is the impulse response (IRF) 
of the first variable (ksotck) to the third variable (mvalue) in {depvarlist} (kstock invest mvalue).{p_end}

{p 4 4}{inp:. pvar2 kstock invest mvalue, lag(2) monte reps(1000)}{p_end}

{p 4 4} Thus, you can type the following commands to redraw the IRFS of {hilite:{it:kstock}} to {hilite:{it:mvalue}}. 
Option {opt scheme(s1mono)} makes Stata draw a black-white figure:{p_end}

{p 4 4}{inp:. graph use gr1_2.gph, scheme(s1mono)}{p_end}

{p 4 4} and then type the following commands to save the graph as {cmd:.wmf} files, 
which can be acceptted and edited easily in Microsoft Word.{p_end}

{p 4 4}{inp:. graph export fig_kstock_invest.wmf, replace}{p_end}

{p 4 4} You can also combine gr1_1.gph, gr1_2.gph, and gr1_3.gph to present the 
IRFs of {it:kstock} to {it:kstock, invest, mvalue} using {help graph combine} command, e.g..{p_end}

{p 4  4} {inp:. graph combine gr1_1.gph gr1_2.gph gr1_3.gph,     ///} {p_end}
{p 12 4} {inp:        title("IRFs of kstock to other variables") ///} {p_end}
{p 12 4} {inp:        rows(1) xcommon colfirst imargin(small)    ///} {p_end}
{p 12 4} {inp:        scheme(s1color)} {p_end}

  

